Title: Blog about the opinion: United States v. Constantinescu

# From Right-to-Control to Fraudulent-Inducement: Constantinescu Maps Post‑Ciminelli Terrain for Section 1348

## Why this case matters
United States v. Constantinescu is the Fifth Circuit’s first substantial § 1348 (securities fraud) decision after Ciminelli and Kousisis, and it matters because it shows how lower courts will distinguish the Supreme Court’s Ciminelli bar on “right‑to‑control” theories from garden‑variety fraudulent‑inducement frauds in the social‑media era. The decision reverses a district‑court dismissal of a multi‑defendant “pump and dump” indictment and holds that allegations that influencers misrepresented their trading positions to induce followers to buy securities adequately plead that the defendants sought to obtain money or property under § 1348. For prosecutors, defense counsel, and markets, Constantinescu signals that the combination of Ciminelli’s limitation and Kousisis’s endorsement of fraudulent inducement will not insulate social influencers who allegedly trick followers into parting with cash.

## What the Fifth Circuit did
The indictment alleged a classic pump‑and‑dump: influencers purchased securities, posted false and misleading trading claims that induced followers to buy and inflate prices, and then sold for profits—allegedly about $114 million in proceeds. The district court dismissed, concluding the alleged deception deprived followers only of valuable economic information (a right‑to‑control theory) rather than a traditional property interest, and thus ran afoul of Ciminelli.

The Fifth Circuit reversed. It applied well‑worn indictment sufficiency principles—taking allegations as true and asking whether all material elements of § 1348 were pled—and framed the government’s theory as fraudulent inducement, not a right‑to‑control theory. Relying on Kousisis, the court emphasized that a fraudulent‑inducement theory targets the taking of money or property (the follower’s purchase funds), and that Ciminelli does not prohibit prosecutions where the fraud induces a victim to hand over money or property. The court also rejected the defendants’ argument that intent to profit—without an intent to leave victims worse off—failed to allege intent to defraud, again citing Kousisis for the proposition that net pecuniary loss to the victim is not a required element.

## How Constantinescu reads Ciminelli and Kousisis together
The opinion performs a relatively straightforward doctrinal triage. Ciminelli stands for the narrow proposition that the fraud statutes do not protect a “right to valuable economic information” standing alone. Kousisis clarifies that a fraudulent‑inducement theory, by contrast, is focused on money or property obtained through deception and is therefore a proper predicate for a fraud charge even if the victim ends up with something of market value (e.g., a contract performance, securities) and does not necessarily suffer a net loss.

Constantinescu treats those holdings as complementary rather than conflicting: Ciminelli rejects a property theory that treats access to information as the property; Kousisis confirms that inducing a party to part with money or other property by material misrepresentations is prosecutable regardless of whether the fraudster intended merely to enrich himself rather than to wreck the victim’s finances. The Fifth Circuit therefore reads the indictment’s allegations of induced purchases as pleading the requisite “money or property” that § 1348 protects.

## What counts as “money or property” under § 1348 after Constantinescu
Constantinescu reinforces a pragmatic reading: the money used by followers to buy securities is the paradigmatic “money or property” the statute protects. The opinion also reiterates two important corollaries drawn from Kousisis and prior wire/mail fraud cases. First, a fraudulent inducement is not defeated by the fact that the victim receives something of value in exchange; the crime is complete when a defendant obtains money or property by materially false pretenses. Second, it is unnecessary at the indictment stage to plead that victims suffered net pecuniary loss—the government need only allege that the fraudulent inducement caused victims to part with money or property.

That said, Constantinescu does not expand § 1348 into protecting every type of informational loss. The court was careful to identify the allegation as a straight inducement to purchase (money transfers), not an attempt to control access to confidential information or the right to make a discretionary choice divorced from a transfer of property—a scenario Ciminelli condemned.

## Practical implications for prosecutors and defense counsel
For prosecutors, Constantinescu offers a clear roadmap for drafting indictments in influencer‑led market‑manipulation cases post‑Ciminelli. To avoid the right‑to‑control pitfall, indictments should expressly allege:
- that the defendant made material misrepresentations or omissions,
- that those statements were intended to induce followers to purchase securities (i.e., to hand over money or property),
- that the defendants actually sought to and did obtain money or property as a result (traceable proceeds help), and
- indicia of intent such as coordinated timing of purchases and posts, secret sales (“dumps”), and profits derived from sales.

Concrete allegations tying deceptive statements to followers’ purchases and to defendants’ proceeds will distinguish fraudulent inducement from a theory premised only on informational control.

For defenders, Constantinescu highlights the contours of viable pretrial attacks. A successful dismissal under Ciminelli still remains possible where an indictment truly rests only on a right‑to‑control theory—where the government’s theory is that the defendant deprived a victim only of information or influence without alleging that the victim transferred money or property. After Constantinescu, motions should press factual insufficiency (causation between posts and purchases), challenge the specificity of alleged misstatements and materiality, and explore factual predicates for scienter (good‑faith belief in trading positions, lack of deceptive intent). First Amendment arguments—couched as protecting opinions or puffery—remain available in appropriate cases, though Constantinescu makes clear they do not automatically immunize alleged fraudulent inducements.

## Downstream effects and open questions
Constantinescu is likely to encourage more aggressive prosecutorial targeting of social‑media‑based manipulation, particularly where indictments trace follower purchases to misleading posts and alleged seller profits. Lower courts will now have to parse factual pleadings carefully to determine whether a given case is a bona fide fraudulent inducement (permissible) or a disguised right‑to‑control claim (forbidden by Ciminelli). That fact‑sensitive line‑drawing could produce more motions to dismiss and interlocutory appeals in closely framed cases.

Several open questions remain. First, how will courts deal with cases where alleged misstatements are of opinion or “puffery” rather than concrete factual claims about holdings and trades? Second, what showing of causation between public posts and discrete purchases will suffice—especially given the diffuse and rapid nature of social‑media trading? Third, how will courts handle cases involving coordinated promotion where some purchasers may have been sophisticated traders who nonetheless relied on the defendants’ posts? These issues will shape the contours of enforcement going forward.

## Bottom line
Constantinescu does not rewrite fraud law; it applies Ciminelli and Kousisis pragmatically to modern market manipulation. The Fifth Circuit makes clear that when social‑media influencers are alleged to have lied to induce followers to buy securities—and thereby obtain their money—a § 1348 indictment can survive a motion to dismiss. The decision provides a useful blueprint for prosecutors seeking to avoid Ciminelli’s snares and a reminder to defense counsel that factual particularity, causation, and scienter will be the battlegrounds in the next wave of influencer‑driven securities‑fraud litigation.



# No Harm, Still Foul: Profit Motive Satisfies Section 1348’s Intent After Kousisis

## Why this case matters

United States v. Constantinescu is the Fifth Circuit’s quick, clarifying answer to a contemporary question about securities fraud in the age of social-media “influencers.” At base, the panel held that an indictment charging a classic “pump-and-dump” scheme survives a motion to dismiss so long as it alleges a deceptive scheme that sought to obtain money or property through fraudulent inducement — even if victims ultimately received market-value securities and even if the defendants sought profit rather than to leave investors worse off. The ruling operationalizes two recent Supreme Court decisions — Ciminelli (drawing a bright line against right-to-control/information-only theories) and Kousisis (confirming that fraud need not be aimed at leaving a victim economically worse off) — and signals that § 1348 prosecutions of traders and online promoters remain viable where the indictment alleges that deception induced purchases.

## The court’s reasoning in brief

The government’s superseding indictment alleged that defendants with large social-media followings bought thinly traded stocks, posted false or misleading messages to induce followers to buy (thereby inflating price), and then sold into that artificially inflated market — realizing about $114 million in profits. The district court dismissed, concluding the indictment alleged only an intent to deprive followers of valuable economic information rather than any traditional property interest. The Fifth Circuit reversed.

The panel’s analysis turned on two doctrinal touchstones. First, Ciminelli forbids a conviction based solely on the deprivation of a nontraditional “right to valuable economic information” — the so-called right-to-control theory — because information itself is not a traditional property interest. Second, Kousisis makes clear that fraud under the federal statutes can be grounded in the fraudulent-inducement theory: a defendant who uses material misstatements to trick a victim into a contract that requires handing over money or property can be guilty even if the scheme does not leave the victim economically worse off. Applying those precedents, the Fifth Circuit read the indictment as alleging fraudulent inducement — i.e., the defendants’ misrepresentations induced followers to hand over money to buy securities — and therefore as alleging deprivation of money or property sufficient to state an offense under § 1348. The court also relied on Baker and related precedents to reject any “mirror-image” requirement that a defendant’s gain must precisely equal the victim’s loss.

## Why the panel’s approach is persuasive

The Fifth Circuit’s posture is careful and doctrinally consistent. It honors Ciminelli’s limit on information-based theories while giving full effect to Kousisis’s recognition that the object of fraud is obtaining money or property, not necessarily producing net pecuniary loss. That alignment matters: prosecutors must plead that deception induced a transfer of money or property (fraudulent inducement), but they need not show at the pleading stage that the victim suffered net economic harm after accounting for what was received in return. This is the pragmatic reading of the statutes — fraud targets wrongful acquisition by deceit, and the acquisition itself is the statutory evil even if the victim receives something nominally of value in return.

The decision also correctly treats the indictment in the light most favorable to the government, as the law requires. At the motion-to-dismiss stage, whether followers in fact relied or whether the market ultimately reflected fair value are factual questions for trial, not pleading-stage dispositives. The court thus preserves the government’s pathway to prove a classic “pump-and-dump” theory at trial while still leaving room for robust factual and legal defenses.

## Doctrinal implications and likely downstream effects

Constantinescu has a number of practical and doctrinal consequences.

First, it reaffirms that post-Ciminelli prosecutions cannot rest on information-property theories but that they can proceed where the government alleges that lies induced purchases (fraudulent inducement). That narrows, but does not hollow out, prosecutorial options: prosecutors must craft indictments that allege deceptive communications and that those communications were intended to, and did, induce tangible transfers (money spent to buy securities).

Second, the decision underscores Kousisis’s substantive point that intent to “harm” the victim is not a statutory prerequisite. Proof that the defendant had money or property in mind (i.e., intended to obtain money through deception) suffices for the mens rea element. That will be particularly important in the social-media context, where promoters often claim they did not intend to leave followers worse off — they sought profit, which Kousisis and now Constantinescu indicate is a sufficient object for a fraud prosecution.

Third, the ruling foreshadows increased supervisory scrutiny of how courts assess allegations about inducement, reliance, and causation at the pleading stage. Although Constantinescu clears a pleading hurdle, it does not eliminate later defenses. Expect defense strategies to press on particularity (Rule 9(b)), factual reliance, causation (did the misrepresentations actually cause followers to buy?), and scienter (did the speaker actually know a statement was false or act recklessly?). Prosecutors will need to allege the details of posts, timing, and contemporaneous trades with sufficient specificity to survive motions to dismiss and to persuade juries.

Fourth, there are practical market implications. Social-media-driven trading has been an enforcement priority for years, and Constantinescu gives prosecutors a clearer doctrinal path to pursue influencers and microcap promoters who orchestrate price runs. The ruling should encourage charges in similar fact patterns, and appellate courts — and perhaps the Supreme Court — may be asked to refine the contours of “inducement” and how closely proximate causation must be tied to deceptive speech.

Finally, the decision brushes up against free-speech sensibilities. The court properly stayed within fraud doctrine, focusing on false and misleading statements made to induce purchases. But as prosecutions increase, courts will need to continue distinguishing protected opinion or market commentary from actionable false statements made with deceptive intent. That line will be hotly litigated and fact-intensive.

## Bottom line

Constantinescu is a reminder that the modern fraud statutes protect the integrity of transfers — not just victims’ informational prerogatives. After Ciminelli closed the door on information-as-property theories, Kousisis opened a robust window for fraudulent-inducement prosecutions, and the Fifth Circuit has walked through that window here. The upshot is straightforward: an objective to obtain money or property by deception is sufficient mens rea under § 1348, even when victims receive market-value securities or when defendants claim they only sought profit. But the decision does not end the debate; it simply moves it from the pleadings phase to the trial phase, where fact, particularity, and proof will determine how far criminal securities enforcement can — and should — go in policing influencer-driven markets.



# Finfluencers in the Dock: Material Misstatements on Social Media After Constantinescu

Why this matters: The Fifth Circuit’s reversal in United States v. Constantinescu brings a high‑stakes, social‑media reality check for “finfluencers.” The court held that a traditional securities‑fraud theory — fraudulent inducement to obtain money or property — adequately supports an indictment alleging that influencers “pumped” microcap stocks by posting false or misleading social‑media statements and then “dumped” positions for profit. That, not the more attenuated “right‑to‑control” theory rejected in Ciminelli, is the hook the government must plead and prove. The decision signals that prosecutors can pursue social‑media traders under 18 U.S.C. § 1348 so long as their allegations point to concrete inducement to buy (i.e., the transfer of money or property), and not merely to the withholding of useful information.

What the opinion does (and doesn’t) say: The Fifth Circuit reviewed the indictment de novo, accepted the allegations as true for pleading purposes, and held that the superseding indictment sufficiently alleged both a scheme to defraud and the requisite intent to defraud. The court relied chiefly on the Supreme Court’s recentClarifications in Ciminelli (rejecting right‑to‑control as a standalone theory) and Kousisis (accepting the fraudulent‑inducement theory and holding that a defendant need not intend to leave the victim worse off overall). Because Constantinescu alleged that the influencers posted materially false statements to induce their followers to purchase securities — and that the defendants covertly sold into the price run‑up, realizing roughly $114 million — the Fifth Circuit concluded the indictment pleaded the deprivation of a protected property interest (money/property) rather than only the loss of informational control.

Parsing the doctrinal lines: opinion and puffery, fact and intent

- Puffery and opinion remain conceptually distinct from actionable misstatements. The court’s logic follows long‑standing fraud doctrine: statements of present fact about holdings, past trades, or present intent (e.g., “I own X shares” or “I will not sell”) are closer to verifiable factual claims and therefore more readily actionable when they are alleged to be false. By contrast, expression of generalized optimism, unverifiable “to the moon” price calls, or hyperbolic endorsements are the classic examples of puffery and nonactionable opinion. Constantinescu does not redraw these general lines, but it illuminates where prosecutors will try to draw them in the social‑media context.

- The special potency of misstatements about present holdings or intent to sell. The indictment’s core allegations — that defendants misrepresented their trading positions and price prospects while secretly selling — are the paradigmatic fraudulent‑inducement scenario: an influencer allegedly tells followers to buy while simultaneously offloading shares. Those sorts of affirmative, contemporaneous factual assertions are inherently capable of inducing purchases and thus of obtaining money or property. Constantinescu underscores that lies about what you currently hold, have bought, or plan to do with your position are legally significant because they bear directly on the audience’s decision whether to part with money to buy a security.

- Omissions and “opinion” that becomes misleading. Although Constantinescu focuses on affirmative misstatements, established fraud principles (as applied in securities and wire/mail‑fraud contexts) caution that an ostensibly opinionated statement can be rendered fraudulent by omissions that make it misleading. A boast that “I’m bullish and holding” coupled with the omission that the speaker has already sold, or that the speaker is being paid to tout the stock, can convert an opinion into a materially misleading communication if that omission is the kind of information a reasonable investor would consider important. The Fifth Circuit’s opinion implicitly points in that direction by emphasizing deceit that induced purchases; whether omissions will support liability in any given case will turn on materiality and the surrounding context.

Intent to defraud and the absence of a required net loss: Kousisis supplied the court’s helpful doctrinal backbone. The Supreme Court there made clear that fraudulent inducement targets money/property even if the victim does not ultimately end up economically worse off overall. Constantinescu leans on that principle to reject the defense argument that an influencer who intended to profit (and perhaps even provided buyers with market‑value shares) lacked the intent element. For prosecutors, that removes a potential pleading obstacle: allege inducement to spend money, allege deceptive intent, and liability can follow even if the defendant gave something of value back or the victim’s net position is not worse in a zero‑sum sense.

What this means for influencers, platforms, and regulators

- For influencers: The practical takeaway is straightforward and stark. Publicly asserting concrete facts about your positions, trades, or intentions while secretly trading against your audience invites legal risk if those assertions are false or materially misleading. Vague optimism and non‑specific commentary are safer from a fraud‑prosecution perspective, but not immune: the more an influencer styles a claim as fact — or omits material realities that would defeat the impression of a bona fide recommendation — the closer it comes to actionable fraud. Disclosure of positions and conflicts of interest is not merely good practice; it is a critical risk‑mitigation measure.

- For platforms and compliance officers: Expect heightened scrutiny. Platforms that host securities commentary may face pressure (regulatory and reputational) to develop clearer policies around paid promotions, position disclosures, and rapid coordination that can produce pump‑and‑dump dynamics. Civil and criminal enforcers alike will look for patterns of coordinated posting, contemporaneous sales, and evidence of intent to profit.

- For prosecutors and plaintiffs’ counsel: Constantinescu confirms a path forward under § 1348: plead a fraudulent‑inducement theory with allegations that the speaker made materially false statements or omissions intended to induce purchases, and that the purchases resulted in the transfer of money or property that the defendant sought to obtain. The Fifth Circuit’s approach sets a pleading standard that is workable at the indictment stage, but conviction will still require proof beyond a reasonable doubt of materiality, causation, and mens rea.

Downstream challenges and unresolved questions

- Materiality in the social‑media ecosystem. Determining materiality on platforms like Twitter, TikTok, or Discord may be messy. Courts will have to grapple with whether concise, ephemeral posts plausibly contain facts that would have significantly altered the total mix of information available to a reasonable investor. Context will matter: who the influencer is, how followers use the information, and whether the posts conveyed verifiable factual claims versus mere enthusiasm.

- Causation and market dynamics. At trial, defendants will press causal defenses: the market price may move for many reasons, and proving that an individual influencer’s posts induced particular purchases (or the flow of funds the defendant obtained) can be factually difficult, especially in liquid or widely followed securities. Pump‑and‑dump cases will be strongest in microcap or thinly traded stocks where a few voices can move price and where contemporaneous sales by the promoter are traceable.

- First Amendment friction. Criminal prosecutions for false statements about securities implicate free‑speech concerns, but the fraud statutes have long been construed to reach materially deceptive commercial speech. Constantinescu does not resolve any broader constitutional challenge; it simply affirms that materially false or misleading commercial assertions used to induce purchases fall within the fraud statutes. Challenges grounded in overbreadth or vagueness may arise in close cases — for example, penalizing hyperbolic or low‑verifiability statements — which will likely be litigated as these facts work through the courts.

Practical litigation pointers

Defendants are likely to attack indictments on familiar grounds: contesting that the charged statements were mere opinion or puffery, arguing a lack of specific intent to deceive, disputing materiality and causation, and pointing to contemporaneous disclosures or market evidence undermining inducement. Prosecutors will try to assemble digital timelines showing posts coincident with purchases, trace the defendants’ sales, and leverage payment or coordination evidence. Because Constantinescu was decided at the pleading stage, watch for how lower courts treat evidentiary development on these fronts.

Conclusion: A narrower but meaningful tool

Constantinescu does not make every bullish tweet a crime. Rather, it confirms that classical fraudulent‑inducement law — not the disfavored “right‑to‑control” theory — is the proper vehicle for prosecuting pump‑and‑dump schemes executed through social media. The ruling sharpens the risk picture for influencers who make concrete, factlike assertions about positions and intentions while secretly monetizing the resulting buying pressure. For lawyers, platforms, and policymakers, the case frames the task ahead: delineating the boundary between actionable deception and protected expression in an era when a single post can move markets.



# Indictments, Not Trials: The Fifth Circuit Tightens the Standard for Pretrial Dismissals

## Why this case matters

United States v. Constantinescu is a timely reaffirmation of a basic, but often forgotten, principle of federal criminal procedure: at the dismissal-on-the-pleadings stage, courts must accept the allegations of an indictment as true and confine their review to whether those allegations, if accepted, state the material elements of the charged offense. The Fifth Circuit reversed a district court that had dismissed a securities‑fraud indictment against a group of social‑media traders accused of a “pump and dump,” reasoning that the district court impermissibly asked for more than Rule 12 allows. In doing so, the panel navigated the post‑Ciminelli/Kousisis landscape—holding that allegations that defendants induced followers to buy securities by false statements adequately plead a fraudulent‑inducement scheme under 18 U.S.C. § 1348, even though the victims received securities that were, at least nominally, worth something.

The decision matters for two overlapping audiences. For prosecutors, it preserves the ability to charge schemes that exploit influencer platforms to induce purchases. For defense counsel, it signals that early dismissal is a steep hill: indictments alleging fraudulent inducement will survive facial attack so long as they allege deception and an object of obtaining money or property. The practical knock‑on: many fights that defendants used to try to win on pleadings will now have to be litigated later—through discovery, motions in limine, Rule 29 motions, and at trial.

## The court’s reasoning in brief

The Fifth Circuit’s analysis is tidy and doctrinally anchored. It began with the familiar pleading sufficiency rubric—Rafoi’s de novo review of indictments, the command to accept indictment allegations as true (Fontenot), and the basic content requirements for indictments (Gaytan; Guzman‑Ocampo). Against that procedural baseline, the panel confronted the doctrinal knots left by two recent Supreme Court decisions.

First, Ciminelli held that the federal fraud statutes do not reach schemes that deprive victims only of "valuable economic information" (the “right‑to‑control” theory). The Fifth Circuit acknowledged Ciminelli’s limit, but explained that Ciminelli does not invalidate the fraudulent‑inducement theory—where a defendant uses a material misstatement to trick a victim into handing over money or property. The indictment in Constantinescu alleged precisely that: defendants posted false and misleading information to induce followers to buy securities, and then sold into those inflated markets. Those allegations, the panel held, describe a scheme to cause followers to part with money or property, not merely to withhold information.

Second, Kousisis clarified that fraudulent‑inducement prosecutions do not require proof that the defendant intended to leave the victim worse off financially; it is enough that obtaining the victim’s money or property was an object of the fraud. The Fifth Circuit relied on Kousisis to reject a related defense contention here—that the indictment fails because defendants intended only to profit for themselves rather than to harm followers. Under Kousisis, intent to obtain money (and a scheme to induce the victim to hand it over) suffices at the pleading stage.

On that footing, the panel concluded the indictment alleged both the scheme and the requisite intent to defraud and reversed the district court.

## Putting the opinion in context: what it permits—and what it forbids

Constantinescu performs two useful gatekeeping functions. First, it enforces the structural rule that Rule 12 dismissal is not a substitute for a pretrial merits inquiry. When an indictment alleges elements of deception and an object of obtaining money or property, courts should not try to evaluate competing inferences about the facts on a motion to dismiss. Second, it clarifies that Ciminelli and Kousisis are complementary: Ciminelli draws a line against right‑to‑control theories where no money or property is alleged to change hands; Kousisis confirms that where the fraud aims to induce the transfer of money or property, the government need not show an intent to cause net loss.

But the opinion is not a blank check. The Fifth Circuit takes care to say that Ciminelli’s exclusion of right‑to‑control theories remains binding; prosecutions that rest solely on an allegation that defendants deprived victims of valuable information without alleging any scheme to obtain money or property will not survive. That is the limiting principle that preserves a meaningful boundary for fraud law and addresses some First Amendment anxieties: false speech that merely withholds or manipulates information cannot be equated to taking a property interest unless the indictment alleges that the speech was used to procure money or property.

## Practical consequences for defense strategy

For defense counsel, Constantinescu should prompt a recalibration of expectations and tactics. The decision nudges several fights later in the case lifecycle.

First, front‑loading dismissal motions based on interpretive arguments about the statute or Ciminelli will be less likely to succeed where the indictment charges fraudulent inducement. Appreciable chances remain for Rule 12 dismissal only where the indictment facially relies on the right‑to‑control theory or otherwise plainly fails to allege one of the statutory elements.

Second, the action shifts to the discovery and evidence phases. If dismissal is unlikely, defense teams should invest in aggressive pretrial discovery and evidentiary work: early, focused requests for particularity and Brady material; motions to compel; targeted Daubert and evidentiary challenges; and motions in limine designed to control the narrative the government may present to a jury. The issue of whether the government can prove fraudulent inducement—whether misleading statements were material, whether followers actually relied, and whether the defendants had the requisite intent—are quintessential merits issues suitable for a trial (or a Rule 29 motion after the government rests).

Third, defense counsel should be prepared to press on jury instructions and preserved appellate arguments. Because the statutory contours remain shaped by Ciminelli and Kousisis, careful instruction drafting that highlights the constitutional and doctrinal limits of the fraud statutes will be vital. Where appropriate, defense teams should preserve arguments that the evidence fails to show that defendants sought to obtain money or property—as opposed to merely inflating prices through speech—or that the alleged misrepresentations were not material to a reasonable investor.

Finally, a practical adjunct: plea discussions. When an indictment survives a Rule 12 motion, the calculus of proceeding to trial—particularly in complex, expensive securities prosecutions—changes. Defendants and counsel should evaluate whether early negotiated resolutions are preferable to protracted, high‑stakes litigation, while bearing in mind the preservation of appellate issues.

## Broader doctrinal and policy implications

Constantinescu will likely be cited for the proposition that post‑Ciminelli challenges do not allow courts to collapse indictment review into a mini‑trial. Circuits already interpreting the post‑Ciminelli/Kousisis framework may find in the Fifth Circuit’s opinion a useful formulation: Ciminelli establishes what is off the table (rights‑to‑control claims); Kousisis clarifies what remains on the table (fraudulent inducement to obtain money or property), and indictment sufficiency simply asks whether the indictment, taken as true, pleads the latter.

The opinion also signals continued prosecutorial appetite for addressing securities misconduct driven by social‑media platforms and influencers. Prosecutors will see Constantinescu as encouragement that an indictment that alleges pumping and dumping through false or misleading social‑media posts is likely to survive early dismissal and reach fact development. That, in turn, may produce more cases—forcing courts to grapple at trial with hard questions about causation, reliance in a market context, and reliance proxies in mass‑market schemes.

At the same time, the decision preserves a guardrail for speech‑related prosecutions by reaffirming that mere manipulation of information—if it does not intentionally induce the transfer of money or property—remains outside the fraud statutes. Whether that guardrail will prove sufficiently protective in practice depends on how carefully trial courts instruct juries and how rigorously appellate courts police the line between protected speech and inducements to part with property.

## Conclusion

United States v. Constantinescu reads like a useful corrective: it reminds trial courts to honor the modest, element‑focused role of a Rule 12 dismissal and to leave disputed factual inferences for later stages. For defendants in modern, media‑driven securities cases, the ruling reduces the probability of winning on pleadings and increases the importance of constructing persuasive evidentiary and jury‑instruction strategies. For prosecutors, it affirms that the fraudulent‑inducement path to conviction remains viable even where victims receive marketable securities. The case does not erase Ciminelli’s limits, but it does insist that those limits be applied in a way that preserves the ordinary, fact‑bound processes of criminal adjudication.



# Section 1348’s Coming of Age: Borrowing Mail and Wire Fraud Concepts to Police Market Manipulation

## Why Constantinescu matters

The Fifth Circuit’s opinion in United States v. Constantinescu (Oct. 2, 2025) is a clear signal that federal criminal securities enforcement can—and will—reach modern market manipulation conducted through social media under 18 U.S.C. § 1348. The panel reversed a district court that had dismissed a pump‑and‑dump indictment on the ground that the alleged conduct only deprived followers of “valuable economic information,” not a traditional property interest. By leaning on mail‑ and wire‑fraud principles and the Supreme Court’s recent guidance in Ciminelli and Kousisis, the Fifth Circuit held that an indictment alleging fraudulent inducement—misrepresentations that cause investors to hand over money to buy securities—sufficiently pleads a scheme and intent to defraud. That ruling preserves a potent criminal tool for prosecuting influencer‑driven manipulation where the government can allege direct inducement to part with money or property.

## What the court said and how it reasoned

The facts alleged in the superseding indictment are familiar: well‑followed social‑media traders purchase thinly traded stocks, tout them with false and misleading posts to induce followers to buy, inflate prices, then sell for a profit—allegedly yielding roughly $114 million in gains. The district court dismissed, reasoning that the conduct allegedly targeted only followers’ access to information rather than a traditional property interest.

The Fifth Circuit reversed. It framed § 1348’s elements in terms used in its prior caselaw: a scheme to defraud (a material misrepresentation intended to obtain something of value, such as money) and an intent to defraud (an intent to deceive and to cause harm to result from the deceit). The panel relied on two Supreme Court decisions that have recently reshaped the contours of criminal fraud doctrine. Ciminelli rejected the so‑called “right‑to‑control” theory—you cannot criminalize depriving someone of valuable economic information alone because that right is not a traditional property interest. Kousisis, by contrast, upheld the fraudulent‑inducement theory: a defendant who uses a material misstatement to trick a victim into a contract that requires handing over money or property can be prosecuted even if the defendant did not intend to leave the victim worse off overall.

Applying that framework, the Fifth Circuit concluded the indictment here did more than allege mere informational withholding. It charged that defendants intentionally used false posts to induce followers to buy securities—that is, to part with money or property. Under Kousisis, that allegation suffices to plead a scheme to obtain money or property by fraud even if the victims ultimately received marketable securities or were not left net poorer. Thus the dismissal could not stand.

## Doctrinal takeaways

Constantinescu illustrates several doctrinal points worth noting.

First, § 1348 remains a malleable criminal statute largely interpreted through the analogies to mail and wire fraud. The Fifth Circuit explicitly leans on that lineage (and the circuit’s prior wire/mail fraud decisions) to supply the definitional contours of “scheme” and “intent.” That approach gives courts doctrinal tools developed over decades of mail/wire fraud jurisprudence to apply in securities contexts, while the Supreme Court’s recent pruning of certain theories (notably Ciminelli’s rejection of a pure right‑to‑control theory) imposes important limits.

Second, the decision underscores the durable vitality of the fraudulent‑inducement theory. Post‑Kousisis, prosecutors can pursue criminal charges where the heart of the allegation is a misrepresentation that causes victims to hand over money or property—even where the fraudster gives something of value in return or the victim does not suffer an ultimate net loss. That squarely fits the classic pump‑and‑dump scenario: the victims buy the security in reliance on false claims, and the fraudster pockets gains when prices spike.

Third, Constantinescu confirms that the constitutional and common‑law worry that fraud law should not criminalize mere speech remains meaningful—Ciminelli’s limitation survives—but it does not immunize misleading market talk that actually induces purchases. The doctrinal line the courts will enforce is between deceptive inducement (actionable) and mere suppression or manipulation of information (not actionable as property fraud by itself).

## Practical consequences for prosecutors and defense counsel

For prosecutors, the ruling is a validation of § 1348 as a go‑to criminal vehicle in social‑media‑driven manipulation cases. Section 1348 is criminal, operates nationwide, and—importantly—does not require the SEC’s administrative apparatus or reliance on Rule 10b‑5’s civil regime. Where the government can plausibly allege that influencer posts were material misrepresentations aimed at inducing purchases, § 1348 provides a straightforward path to indictment.

That said, the prosecution’s path is not unfettered. The government must craft indictments that allege fraudulent inducement—victims were induced to part with money or property—not merely that defendants distorted the information environment. Thoughtful pleading will emphasize concrete transactions tied to specific misstatements and the defendant’s profit motive, as the Fifth Circuit found sufficient here.

Defense counsel will, predictably, press several lines. One is the Ciminelli line: challenge whether alleged conduct actually deprived anyone of a traditional property interest. Another is First Amendment and overcriminalization concerns—contending that prosecutions that rest on hyperbole, opinionated market commentary, or puffery risk chilling protected speech. Beyond these constitutional claims, defendants will seek narrow jury instructions and dismissal where indictments fail to tie misstatements to particular purchases. The post‑Kousisis landscape also invites factual fights over whether the defendant truly intended to “obtain” money or merely offered something of value in exchange.

Procedurally, Section 1348 prosecutions may offer advantages—criminal penalties, arrest power, and the ability to pursue accessories and conspirators—while also creating risks for prosecutors (jury skepticism about prosecuting speech, potential appellate scrutiny about where the line between speech and fraud lies).

## Broader implications for securities enforcement

Constantinescu will likely accelerate criminal scrutiny of influencer‑based market manipulation. Social media created low‑friction channels for coordinated touting and cross‑promotion; DOJ has already shown interest in policing these dynamics, and this Fifth Circuit ruling removes a doctrinal excuse for dismissal where the indictment ties misstatements to induced purchases.

We should expect several downstream developments. First, prosecutors will lean into careful bridge‑building between specific misstatements and transactional harm—pleading concrete buys tied in time and causation to particular posts. Second, courts will receive more contested motions arguing the boundary between protected commentary and criminal inducement. That is fertile ground for appellate development: how specific and direct must the inducement be, how proximate the causal link, and how to instruct juries about “property” and “intent” in a digital, information‑rich market?

Third, the decision will spur dialogue (and perhaps case law) about appropriate jury instructions and evidentiary limits to prevent criminal liability from swallowing legitimate market discourse. The Supreme Court’s Ciminelli and Kousisis guardrails remain crucial here; prosecutors must not try to recast private informational advantages as property. The tension—protecting markets from fraud while preserving robust speech—will shape litigation posture in the coming years.

Finally, Constantinescu highlights the interplay between criminal enforcement under § 1348 and the SEC’s civil remedies under Rule 10b‑5. The statutes serve complementary roles: the SEC can pursue broad remedial and disgorgement relief; the DOJ can bring criminal charges where the facts permit a fraud‑by‑inducement theory. Expect continued coordination, but also strategic choices by enforcement agencies based on evidentiary posture, deterrent goals, and the need to secure convictions.

## Conclusion

Constantinescu does not open the floodgates to criminalizing mere market commentary. But it does mark an important doctrinal development: courts will treat social‑media pump‑and‑dump schemes as classic objects of § 1348 prosecutions when indictments plead fraudulent inducement—i.e., misrepresentations that caused followers to hand over money or property. In an era when online influencers can move prices quickly, the decision equips prosecutors to use a long‑standing federal fraud framework against modern methods of market manipulation, while preserving the Ciminelli guardrail that mere informational control is not a species of property fraud. Expect hard factual battles over causation, intent, and the line between puffery and deception; those battles will define the contours of criminal securities enforcement in the social‑media age.



# Conspiracy in the Crowd: Group Liability for Online Pump‑and‑Dumps Under Section 1349

## Why this case matters

United States v. Constantinescu (5th Cir. Oct. 2, 2025) is the first major appellate decision to squarely confront how traditional fraud and conspiracy doctrines apply to coordinated securities schemes run through modern social‑media networks. The Fifth Circuit reversed a district court’s dismissal of an indictment charging a band of well‑known online traders with conspiracy and substantive securities fraud for allegedly “pumping” stocks via false posts and then “dumping” shares for profit. In doing so the court relied on the Supreme Court’s recent clarifications in Ciminelli and Kousisis about what counts as protected “property” and what the Government must prove under the fraudulent‑inducement theory. The practical stakes are high: the remand will force the district court to grapple with how to prove agreement and attribution among multiple influencers, how far coconspirator (Pinkerton) liability reaches, and how to manage the avalanche of cross‑platform digital evidence without prejudicing defendants or drowning jurors in noise.

## What the Fifth Circuit held (and did not)

The Fifth Circuit’s holding is narrowly framed but consequential. It reversed dismissal because the indictment, read in the light most favorable to the Government, adequately alleged a fraudulent‑inducement scheme: defendants allegedly used materially false and misleading social‑media communications to induce followers to buy securities, thereby parting with money or property that the defendants sought to obtain. That aligns the indictment with Kousisis’s distinction between a forbidden “right‑to‑control” theory and an adequately protected fraudulent‑inducement theory that targets money or property obtained through deceit. The panel also relied on Kousisis to reject the claim that the Government must show the victims were left economically worse off; obtaining money or property under materially false pretenses suffices.

The court did not resolve how to prove conspiracy in decentralized online markets, did not announce rules for attributing one influencer’s statement to another, and did not map the contours of Pinkerton liability in this particular context. Those are the issues the remand will now surface.

## Agreement in decentralized online spaces: the evidentiary challenge

Conspiracy under 18 U.S.C. § 1349 requires an agreement to commit an offense and, ordinarily, an overt act in furtherance. Proving a meeting of the minds among a loosely connected set of social‑media personalities will be tougher than proving fraud by a single actor. Unlike a classic corporate or organized‑crime setting, influencers often coordinate without formal meetings, using ephemeral DMs, encrypted chat groups, shared schedules, or merely tacitly mimicking one another. On remand the Government will need to show enough of a link—communications, a common plan, shared timing, or coordinated financial benefit—to permit a reasonable jury to infer agreement rather than parallel self‑interested conduct.

Practical evidence that courts will expect to see includes time‑stamped group chats or DMs (Telegram, Discord, WhatsApp), direct messages arranging coordinated posting times or narratives, contemporaneous spreadsheets or deal‑slips showing shared purchase/sale plans, and records of profit‑sharing or centralized token/crypto flows. Absent such documentary breadcrumbs, prosecutors can turn to circumstantial proof: strikingly synchronized posts, identical false narratives appearing across multiple accounts in short order, a pattern of correlated purchases followed by coordinated sell orders, and the unmistakable economic fingerprint of one party buying large positions and later realizing outsized gains while followers transact in the opposite direction.

Defense counsels’ strongest answer will often be that contemporaneous coordination is plausibly explicable by ordinary social dynamics—shared sources, copying popular takes, or independent trading strategies reacting to the same catalyst. The district court will need careful jury instructions distinguishing lawful coordination or parallel intent from conspiratorial agreement.

## Attribution and the problem of “whose speech counts”

A difficult evidentiary and doctrinal question is attribution: when does one influencer’s post become the act of another defendant for purposes of conspiracy and substantive liability? The Fifth Circuit’s opinion rests on the fraudulent‑inducement theory—that victims were induced to buy securities by materially false statements. But in a group case, victims may have seen and acted on posts from different defendants. The Government will therefore need to show that each charged defendant either made (or directed) materially false representations that induced purchases, or that defendants knowingly participated in a common scheme where other coconspirators’ posts were the means of inducement.

Attribution will turn on classic agency and aiding‑and‑abetting principles, plus an assessment of how followers consume content. Evidence that one defendant wrote copy that another reposted, shared drafts across accounts, or coordinated messaging strategies strengthens the Government’s case. So does proof that defendants benefitted from one another’s audience overlap—e.g., that one defendant’s post reliably moved the volume in accounts concentrated among another defendant’s followers. Courts may also scrutinize platform mechanics and analytics: who posted first, the velocity of reposting, whether accounts were linked by management services, and whether defendants used common broker accounts or trading bots.

The district court will also face authentication and hearsay questions when the Government relies on screenshot captures, saved posts, or third‑party records of deleted material. Robust metadata and platform records will be essential to authenticate posts and show timing and reach.

## Pinkerton liability and foreseeability: how broad will coconspirator exposure be?

If the Government proves a conspiracy under § 1349, Pinkerton principles mean coconspirators can be responsible for substantive securities‑fraud acts committed by their partners if those acts were in furtherance of the conspiracy and reasonably foreseeable. In social‑media pump‑and‑dumps, that could sweep broadly: a defendant who agreed to a plan to “pump” could be held liable for another conspirator’s posts or sales that induced purchases, even if the defendant did not personally author them or execute the particular trades.

Two limits will matter. First, foreseeability: courts will ask whether the specific type and timing of the acts were a natural and probable consequence of the agreement. Evidence that defendants expected coordinated multi‑account postings, reciprocal amplification, or shared sell‑off timelines will make foreseeability easy to show. Second, in furtherance: courts will require proof that the substantive acts advanced the common scheme, not mere collateral or independent trading.

Defendants will argue that the online ecosystem makes neither element a straightforward fit for Pinkerton. They will stress individualized causation—followers who saw and followed only one influencer, or trades motivated by market news rather than coordinated messaging. Prosecutors, conversely, will emphasize the economics of audiences, the design of amplification strategies, and empirical patterns tying particular posts to follower behavior.

## Trial management: severance, spillover, and jury complexity

Multi‑defendant trials present real risks of spillover prejudice in this context. Influencers differ in prominence, audience size, and conduct. A star defendant’s inflammatory posts or a coconspirator’s post‑arrest admissions could poison jurors’ views of less‑culpable codefendants. On remand, defense teams will likely press for severance, limiting instructions, and targeted motions to exclude highly prejudicial evidence that is not directly attributable to individual defendants.

From the court’s perspective, practical case management will matter. Staging evidence by defendant or by discrete conspiratorial acts, using limiting instructions, and considering separate trials where one defendant’s conduct far outstrips the others may be necessary to protect fairness. The prosecution will counter that severance may impair proof of the coordinated scheme; courts will have to balance judicial economy against the risk of unfair prejudice.

## The evidentiary toolbox: chat logs, cross‑platform posts, and follower trading data

Proving a conspiracy and causation in online pump‑and‑dumps will be an exercise in forensic synthesis. Chat logs (DMs, group chats) can provide the smoking‑gun evidence of agreement; but such logs must be authenticated, and their potentially voluminous and exculpatory content may complicate trial narratives. Cross‑platform posts—text, video, and audio—show the content and timing of alleged misrepresentations; platform metadata and archived copies will be essential to proving what was said, when, and to whom.

Perhaps most novel will be follower trading data. The Government will likely seek brokerage records (order books, timestamped trades, and account identifiers) and platform analytics (impressions, demographics, click‑through and follow‑through metrics) to show that posts caused spikes in buying among followers and that those spikes preceded defendants’ sell transactions. Correlating social‑media reach with microstructure movements will require expert testimony from data scientists and market‑structure specialists. That evidence raises its own questions: matching anonymous or pseudonymous brokerage accounts to real‑world followers will involve subpoenaing platform and broker records and wrestling with privacy and Fourth Amendment issues. Defense experts will challenge causal inferences, identifying confounders such as contemporaneous news, algorithmic amplification independent of defendants’ intent, or wash trades.

Courts will also confront common evidentiary objections: hearsay (when a witness recounts a post), business‑records exceptions, and authentication of screenshots and archived web content. Judges should expect extensive pretrial litigation over the admissibility and use of such materials.

## Doctrinal ripple effects and open questions

Constantinescu signals that traditional fraud and conspiracy frameworks can reach coordinated social‑media schemes, but it leaves open several doctrinal questions that could shape future litigation. First, how granular must proof of inducement be—must the Government tie specific purchases to specific misrepresentations, or is statistical proof of market‑wide inducement enough? The Fifth Circuit’s reliance on Kousisis suggests that a showing the defendants intended to obtain money via inducement will survive sufficiency challenges, but trial proof will need to bridge from large datasets to individualized causation at least insofar as the jury must find that victims were induced.

Second, the case invites further delineation of the boundary between lawful persuasive speech (including bullish advocacy) and actionable deception when multiple actors amplify messages. That boundary will implicate First Amendment concerns—though neither the Fifth Circuit opinion nor Kousisis upended long‑standing doctrine that knowingly false statements of fact intended to induce financial transactions can give rise to liability.

Third, Constantinescu raises questions about the scope of coconspirator liability when participants occupy different roles: content creators, account managers, promoters, and traders. Courts will need to parse role‑specific culpability and ensure that Pinkerton exposure is not limitless.

Finally, the decision will spur tactical changes in both prosecution and defense practice. Prosecutors will be more likely to indict coordinated influencer networks and will invest in cross‑platform forensic capabilities. Defendants and their counsel will emphasize decentralization, independent motives, and the lack of formal agreement, and will press for early discovery of platform analytics to test causation theories.

## Conclusion

Constantinescu is a pragmatic application of Kousisis and Ciminelli to a modern fraud modality: the social‑media pump‑and‑dump. By reversing dismissal, the Fifth Circuit permits the Government to try to prove that a group of influencers agreed to a fraudulent plan and used social media to obtain money under false pretenses. The remand will be a proving ground for how courts translate ancient conspiracy and Pinkerton doctrines into a world of DMs, cross‑posted clips, and algorithmic amplification. How district courts manage attribution, foreseeability, and the admission of complex digital evidence will determine whether prosecutions succeed without unfairly sweeping in independent actors or suppressing lawful speech. Practitioners on both sides should expect protracted pretrial skirmishing over coordination evidence, severance, and expert analysis of the social‑media‑to‑trade causal chain.